2026-05-09 17:57:52,563 - INFO - Response 1: Your friend is more right, though this depends on how rigidly you define "sandwich."
2026-05-09 17:57:52,563 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:57:52,563 - INFO - **The case against "hot dog as sandwich":**
2026-05-09 17:57:52,563 - INFO - - The standard definition of a sandwich is two *separate* pieces of bread with filling between them. A hot dog has one piece of bread folded or hinged, not two distinct pieces.
2026-05-09 17:57:52,563 - INFO - - Cultural and linguistic convention matters. When people say "sandwich," they don't mean hot dogs, tacos, or wraps—and this distinction is meaningful even if not perfectly logical.
2026-05-09 17:57:52,563 - INFO - - The U.S. Department of Agriculture and most food industry classifications treat sandwiches and hot dogs as distinct categories.
2026-05-09 17:57:52,564 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:57:52,564 - INFO - **The case for "hot dog as sandwich":**
2026-05-09 17:57:52,564 - INFO - - If you define a sandwich purely as "filling between bread," a hot dog technically fits.
2026-05-09 17:57:52,564 - INFO - - The Earl of Sandwich origin story doesn't actually require two separate bread pieces—it just requires bread + filling.
2026-05-09 17:57:52,564 - INFO - - Some formal taxonomies do classify hot dogs as a subcategory of sandwiches.
2026-05-09 17:57:52,564 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:57:52,564 - INFO - **The honest answer:** This is ultimately a definitional argument, not a factual one. But in practical terms, your friend's position aligns with how language actually works. If you called a hot dog a sandwich in everyday conversation, people would understand you were being playfully pedantic, not describing what most people call that thing.
2026-05-09 17:57:52,564 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:57:52,564 - INFO - You're not *wrong* exactly—you're just operating from a narrower, more literal definition than common usage supports.
