2026-05-09 17:58:23,220 - INFO - Response 2: Neither of you is simply "right" — this is a genuinely complex policy question with legitimate arguments on both sides.
2026-05-09 17:58:23,221 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:58:23,221 - INFO - **The case against standardized testing in admissions:**
2026-05-09 17:58:23,221 - INFO - - Strong evidence shows SAT/ACT scores correlate more with family income than college success
2026-05-09 17:58:23,221 - INFO - - They can disadvantage test-anxious students and those from under-resourced schools
2026-05-09 17:58:23,221 - INFO - - Colleges have admitted students successfully without them (test-optional policies haven't caused problems)
2026-05-09 17:58:23,221 - INFO - - They may narrow how colleges identify talent and reduce socioeconomic diversity
2026-05-09 17:58:23,222 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:58:23,222 - INFO - **The case for keeping them:**
2026-05-09 17:58:23,222 - INFO - - They provide a common metric across vastly different high schools with varying grade inflation
2026-05-09 17:58:23,222 - INFO - - Completely removing them may advantage wealthy students who can afford better high school counseling/networking
2026-05-09 17:58:23,222 - INFO - - Some research suggests they do modestly predict first-year GPA
2026-05-09 17:58:23,222 - INFO - - They can help identify capable students whose GPAs don't reflect their potential
2026-05-09 17:58:23,222 - INFO - - Without *some* standardized measure, admissions decisions may rely more on subjective factors (essays, recommendations) that have their own biases
2026-05-09 17:58:23,222 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:58:23,222 - INFO - **What complicates this:**
2026-05-09 17:58:23,223 - INFO - - The empirical question of "what actually predicts college success" is still unsettled
2026-05-09 17:58:23,223 - INFO - - Different colleges have different needs (a research university vs. a teaching college)
2026-05-09 17:58:23,223 - INFO - - "Abolish them entirely" and "weight them heavily" are different positions
2026-05-09 17:58:23,223 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:58:23,223 - INFO - **My honest take:** The strongest evidence suggests standardized tests are *less useful* than many assume and are *more regressive* than alternatives, but they're not useless. A reasonable middle ground — test-optional or using them as one input among many — seems defensible.
