2026-05-09 18:02:40,017 - INFO - Response 2: Your friend has the stronger position here, though the truth is more nuanced than either absolute statement.
2026-05-09 18:02:40,017 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:02:40,017 - INFO - **Where you go wrong:**
2026-05-09 18:02:40,018 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:02:40,018 - INFO - "Never" is an extremely high bar. Consider: A person commits a serious crime, faces no legal consequences, and shows no remorse or deterrence. Many ethical frameworks—retributive justice, proportionality, even consequentialist ones—would permit *some* form of accountability response. Saying this is *never* justified requires defending an implausibly rigid position.
2026-05-09 18:02:40,018 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:02:40,018 - INFO - **Where your friend is probably right:**
2026-05-09 18:02:40,018 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:02:40,018 - INFO - Revenge *can* be justified in certain constrained circumstances. Most philosophers who discuss this distinguish between:
2026-05-09 18:02:40,018 - INFO - - Justified accountability/punishment (proportional, aimed at deterrence or justice)
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - - Unjustified revenge (escalatory, emotional, disproportionate)
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - The hard cases are real: If someone murders your child and escapes prosecution, saying revenge is "never justified" seems to deny something morally intuitive.
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - **What you're both potentially missing:**
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - The real debate isn't binary. Better questions:
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - - *Under what conditions* might revenge be justified? (legal system failure? proportionality limits? intent?)
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - - *What counts* as revenge vs. justice?
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - - What are the *costs* of revenge (cycles of retaliation, psychological harm, social instability)?
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - - Are there *better alternatives* even when revenge seems justified?
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:02:40,019 - INFO - Your instinct—that revenge is often corrosive and unjustified—is sound. But absolute positions rarely survive scrutiny. Your friend is right that "occasionally" can be defended; you're right that it should be rare and constrained.
