2026-05-09 18:03:56,671 - INFO - Response 2: Your friend is right, though the fuller answer is more nuanced than either position stated simply.
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - **Why games qualify as art:**
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - Video games meet the basic criteria applied to other recognized art forms. They're creative works that combine multiple media (visual design, music, narrative, interactive systems) to evoke aesthetic and emotional responses. Many games are intentionally designed with artistic vision—think *Journey*, *The Last of Us*, *Gris*, or *Kentucky Route Zero*. They're exhibited in museums, studied in academic art programs, and feature work by artists with clear creative intent.
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - The "interactivity" argument—that this somehow disqualifies games—doesn't hold. Theater is interactive (audience response affects the room's energy). Literature requires reader interpretation. The audience's participation doesn't make these less art.
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - **What makes this complicated:**
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - Not all games are art in equal measure. Some are primarily entertainment products with no artistic ambition, and that's fine—not all films or books are art either, some are just products. The category "video games" is broad enough to include both.
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - The real debate isn't really "are games art?" but rather "which games are art, and by what standards?" That's true of every medium.
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 18:03:56,672 - INFO - **Where you might be getting traction:** If you're arguing against a narrow definition of art that requires certain traditional forms, or against pretension about games, there's something defensible there. But the blanket claim that *no* video games are art doesn't survive scrutiny.
